    "body": "Early work on the KSM mine in B.C. Credit: Seabridge Gold\nPosted By: Colin McClelland November 24, 2026\nSeabridge Gold’s (TSX: SEA; NYSE: SA) bid to build one of Canada’s largest undeveloped gold projects hit another setback when a British Columbia court struck down the decision that kept an environmental approval from expiring.\nB.C. must undertake deep consultation with the Tsetsaut Skii km Lax Ha Nation before it decides again whether construction at the KSM project had “substantially started,” B.C. Supreme Court Justice Emily Burke confirmed Nov. 18. Seabridge shares fell $10.72 , or 29%, to close at $25.92 in Toronto the next day, when the company disclosed the ruling. They were at $27.64 on Monday, valuing the company at $2.99 billion.\n“We are very confident that KSM has more than met the requirements for a Substantially Started designation,” Seabridge CEO Rudi Fronk said in the company’s Nov. 19 statement on the ruling.\nSeabridge faces the review amid competing First Nations claims, while a dispute with Tudor Gold (TSXV: TUD) holds up permits for the tunnels that would carry KSM ore to the mill. It still needs a partner to build the project and has a loan due at year-end.\nLarge potential\nSeabridge’s 2022 prefeasibility study projected 1.03 million oz. of gold a year on average for 33 years. That’s 46% more than the roughly 706,000 oz. that Natural Resources Canada estimates all B.C. mines produced in 2025.\nBurke clarified that her June ruling had quashed B.C.’s 2024 decision on the Golden Triangle copper-gold project, 65 km northwest of Stewart. Under provincial assessment rules , the certificate would stand for the life of the project if B.C. finds enough work took place by July 29 this year. The 2014 certificate remains in force during the review, Seabridge said.\nConstruction spending at KSM had topped $700 million (US$487 million) by July 29, up from $444 million when B.C. made its original decision, Seabridge said. The Nov. 18 drop erased about $1.16 billion in market value. Even after Monday’s 11% rebound, the shares were 25% below their $36.64 close the day before Seabridge disclosed the ruling.\nFirst Nations\nOn Monday, the Nisga’a and Tahltan nations backed the original provincial decision and rejected the Tsetsaut Skii km Lax Ha Nation’s territorial claim at KSM. The project lies in Tahltan territory and in the Nass Area and Nass Wildlife Area covered by the Nisga’a treaty, the two nations said. Their development companies jointly own Treaty Creek Limited Partnership, which worked with BC Hydro on KSM’s power connection.\n“We are the rights holders and decision makers for our respective Territories,” the Nisga’a and Tahltan said in a joint statement on KSM.\nThe Tsetsaut Skii km Lax Ha say the proposed tailings site stretches 9 km across their territory. In September, the nation called for a bonded fund to pay for cleanup and long-term water treatment. It also wants monitors it appoints to have the power to halt work at the site.\n“Consultation means more than allowing us to speak,” Chief Darlene Simpson said at the time. “It means acting on our concerns as stewards of the land.”\nTunnel dispute\nB.C. has deferred a decision on permits to build the full length of the twin Mitchell Treaty tunnels. They would carry ore from the Mitchell Valley to a processing plant in the Treaty Valley. About 12.5 km of the route crosses Tudor’s claims.\nTudor says the proposed route crosses both its Goldstorm deposit and Perfectstorm zone at Treaty Creek. It is challenging the province’s authority to reserve a tunnel corridor across claims Tudor says predate it. Seabridge says it has permits for limited work at the tunnel entrances and plans to finish a feasibility study before starting tunnel construction.\nSeabridge estimated in 2022 that KSM would cost US$6.4 billion to build. It was planning next year’s field program with its preferred joint venture partner while the two negotiated a deal, the company said in its quarterly update this month. Fronk told The Northern Miner last year he expected to name a partner by the end of 2025.\nSeabridge drew the remaining US$50 million on a US$100-million loan on Nov. 2. The full amount comes due Dec. 31 and can be repaid in cash or, with Toronto Stock Exchange approval, in shares, the company said in its quarterly filing this month.\n-With files from David Cassels Print\nCOMMODITY: Gold\nREGION: Canada\nCOMPANY: Seabridge Gold Tudor Gold\nRepublish this article",
